  "answer_to_user": "Here's the raw top-of-the-yield-table right now — but please read the caveat first, because this is exactly where income investors get hurt. The very highest 'yields' in the world are almost all yield traps, not free income. Examples from the screen: Land & Houses REIT (LHRREIT.BK, Thailand) shows ~46.8%, HUYA Inc (HUYA.US) ~45.8% but it CUT its dividend in 2025 and its trailing dividend is down ~95% YoY with negative EPS (-0.07) — a collapsing payout, not a sustainable 46% income stream. NH Prime REIT (338100.KO) shows ~45% but last cut in 2022 with a P/E of 480. Bank of Gansu (2333.HK-area) shows a 37% forward vs 49% trailing yield, meaning a one-off/special dividend inflated the trailing number. A number like 40%+ is almost never a real forward income yield — it's usually a price that collapsed while the last dividend hasn't reset yet, or a one-time special payout. Before acting on any of these you'd want to pair the headline yield with the payout ratio (>1 = paying out more than it earns), the price trend (near 52-week low + bad news = trap), and dividend reliability (no-cut streak, YoY dividend direction). If your real goal is safe income, a 5-7% yield backed by a healthy payout ratio and a long no-cut streak is far more useful than the top of this list. I'm flagging the risk, not telling you to buy or avoid any specific name."
